    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0038",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 38,
    "stem": "Which of the following indicates a poor prognosis\nin a patient with neuroblastoma?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Hyperdiploid DNA"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Absence of N-myc amplification"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Age 1 year"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Age >13 years"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Low serum ferritin levels"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "SIMILAR TO PREVIOUS BOARD EXAM\nCONCEPT/PRINCIPLE. A number of biologic variables\nhave been studied in children with neuroblastoma. An\nopen biopsy is often required to provide sufficient\ntissue for analysis. The presence of hyper-diploid\ntumor DNA is associated with a favorable prognosis,\nwhereas N-myc amplification is associated with a poor\nprognosis regardless of patient age. The Shimada\nclassification describes tumors as having either\nfavorable or unfavorable histologic features based on\nthe degree of differentiation, the mitosis-karyorrhexis\nindex, and the presence or absence of schwannian\nstroma. In general, children of any age with localized\nneuroblastoma and infants 1 year of age with\nadvanced disease and favorable disease\ncharacteristics have a high likelihood of disease free\nsurvival. By contrast, older children with advanced\ndisease have a significantly decreased chance for cure\neven with intensive therapy. For example, aggressive\nmultiagent chemotherapy has resulted in a 2-year\nsurvival rate of approximately 20% in older children\nwith stage IV disease. Neuroblastoma in the\nadolescent has a worse long-term prognosis\nregardless of stage or site and, in many cases, a more\nprolonged course. High serum ferritin levels portend a\nworse prognosis.",
