    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0234",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 234,
    "stem": "A 3 mo old infant has a history of failure to thrive\naccompanied by infrequent bowel movement.\nDuring rectal examination, patient subsequently\npasses large amount of stool. Rectal tone was\nnormal. Which of the following is not needed in\nestablishing the diagnosis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "rectal motility test"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "rectal biopsy"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "barium enema"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "colonoscopy"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "none of the above"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "this is a classic case of hirschprung disease. the gold\nstandard for the diagnosis is rectal biopsy. Showing\nabsence of ganglion. Rectal motility and barium enema\nwill also highly yield a diagnostic clue for the presence\nof this disease. colonoscopy will provide normal result\nas there are no visible mucosal defect in hirschprung.",
