    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0290",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 290,
    "stem": "A 3 year old girl was brough to the ER due to pallor,\nlethargy and decreased urine output. The said\nsymptoms were preceded by a 5 day history of\nbloody diarrhea and fever. Blood tests revealed\nanemia, thrombocytopenia and an elevated\ncreatinine level. What is the most common\ncausative agent that initiated the events that led to\nthe patient's currrent condition ?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Giardia lamblia"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Vibrio cholerae"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Escherichia coli"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Staphylococcus aureus"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Cytomegalovirus"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "The hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS) is the most\ncommon cause of acute renal failure in young children.\nIt is classically characterized by the triad of\nmicroangiopathic hemolytic anemia,\nthrombocytopenia, and uremia.acute enteritis with\ndiarrhea caused by Shiga-like toxin–producing\nEscherichia coli 0157: H7 precedes 80% or more of\nHUS cases",
