    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0595",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 595,
    "stem": "A 24 year old primigravid gave birth to a live term\nbaby boy. On physical examination, the child was\nnoted to have imperforate anus. Patient was\nobserved for 24 hours and there was no noted\npassage of meconium stained urine or the presence\nof meconium at the median raphe. At the 24th hour\nof life a, cross-table lateral x-ray was done and\nshowed that the marker on the skin was 3.5 cm\nfrom the rectum. What is the definitive\nmanagement for this type of impeforate anus?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Perform colostomy immediately"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Simple dilatation with Hegar dilators"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Posterior sagittal anorectoplasty"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Swenson's pull-through procedure"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Kasai procedure"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Children with a high lesion have a double-barrel\ncolostomy performed. This effectively separates the\nfecal stream from the urinary tract. It also allows the\nperformance of an augmented pressure colostogram\nbefore repair to identify the exact position of the distal\nrectum and the fistula. The definitive repair or\nposterior sagittal anorectoplasty (PSARP) is\nperformed at about 1 yr of age.",
